This is a binarized image in which the black areas are copper. The lower trace runs through a pad with a hole. Along the pad's lower edge, around x=250-440, there is a rectangular protrusion about 20 px tall with small notches at its corners. That looks like a pad outline with some irregularity, though it could also be a spur or spurious copper. The left end of the trace at the bottom (x=0-30, y=320-380) shows a rounded corner, which is probably just a curved trace end.

The rectangular bump under the pad is a bit unusual, since the pad would normally be rounded or have a smooth outline. The trace is wide here and the bump is slightly asymmetrical, so I lean toward a defect, but not with high confidence.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.55, "reason": "A rectangular copper protrusion with notched corners extends below the pad on the lower trace, which looks like a spur or spurious copper rather than a clean pad outline."}

This is a binary image in which the black regions are copper. The wide trace has several white holes, which could be pin holes inside the copper. Two holes sit in the wide area, at about (405,503) and (564,467), and a third at (522,632) lies near the edge. All three are small, round and clean.

These could be drill holes or vias, but they are tiny and have no surrounding pad ring. The one at (564,467) sits next to the edge, with a notch at the right edge (~590,470), and the circle at (630,460) looks like a pad. Holes inside a copper trace without annular rings are most likely pin hole defects.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "Small isolated round voids (pin holes) appear inside the wide copper trace, at roughly (405,503) and (564,467)."}

The image is binary, with black as copper and white as substrate. The copper region contains several round white holes: a small one at top left, a larger one at the center top, and a partial one at the left edge. Round white voids inside a copper pad are consistent with pin holes. The larger hole at (290, 100) is about 70 px across, which is too big to be a normal drill hole in a plain copper pour, so it looks more like a defect. The small hole at (32, 65) is also unusual.

The traces themselves look clean.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "Round white voids (pin holes) appear inside the copper area at top-left and center-top, and at the left edge."}

The image is a binary board image with a large copper area (black) and a white diagonal band of substrate. It contains three small white circles. These are probably pin holes in the copper, since they sit inside the large copper region. The circle at the top left is larger, about 30 px across. The two at the bottom right are smaller, about 22 px. They could be intended holes or vias, but in this kind of dataset, isolated round white dots inside a copper pour usually indicate pin holes.

The colors may be inverted, in which case white is copper and the dots are spurious copper. Either way, isolated dots like these suggest a defect.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "Isolated small circular spots inside the large copper region look like pin holes or spurious copper."}

The image is binary. A large black region has two white round holes, which look like pin holes in the copper. The lower right shows a thin black T-shaped line on a white background. Which color is copper is unclear, but the black region with two small white circles suggests the black is copper and the white dots are pin holes. Alternatively the black is the substrate and the white dots are pads or vias, though they sit very close to the edge and are tiny.

The T-shape of thin black lines on white could be a trace on a substrate, in which case white would be the substrate. That reading makes the black large area a copper pour, and the white dots inside it pin holes. It is ambiguous, but the two dots are small and uniform, so I lean toward them being defects.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "Two small round white voids inside the large black copper area look like pin holes."}

I can't tell for certain, but the image looks defective. It is a binary image with white as the substrate and black as copper, or the reverse. The white traces are wide, and the holes in the left black region are white dots, which are probably pads or holes.

Near the holes at (135,153), (135,249) and (135,346) there are thin lines running from the holes toward the white trace edge. One is a small black mark at (190,172). Another is a faint streak at (175,257). A third is a streak from the hole at (135,346) to about x=185. These streaks look like thin spurs or hairline connections between the pad and the trace, which would make them short or spurious copper defects.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.7, "reason": "Thin hairline streaks and a small mark run from the holes toward the trace edge near (190,172), (175,257) and (150-185,346), which look like spurs or spurious copper."}

The image is a binary rendering of a large black copper region, with white pin-hole-like circles inside it at three positions. These are probably through-holes or pads, and they are evenly spaced and nearly identical in size, which suggests they are intentional. There is also a small black triangle at the top edge near x=160, which may just be a trace corner that is mostly out of frame.

It is unclear whether the white dots are intended features or pin-hole defects. In this kind of dataset, defects are usually small, and three evenly spaced round holes of the same size look like design features such as vias. The region has no visible mousebite, short, or spur, so I judge the board non-defective, though with low confidence.

{"defective": false, "confidence": 0.55, "reason": "The three evenly spaced, uniform round holes in the copper look like intentional vias or holes, and no open, short, mousebite, spur, or spurious copper is visible."}

The image is a binary view with a thick black trace running across the lower part and turning at the right. Below it are two black round features, a larger dot and a smaller oval dot, at the bottom edge. They could be pin holes in white copper, or they could be pads or vias. The trace edges look smooth, with no visible mousebite, spur, short or open.

The smaller oval dot at the bottom is the one uncertain feature. It is isolated and could be a hole in the copper. But the region below the trace appears to be white substrate or copper, and I can't tell which. If it is copper, the dots would be holes or pin holes. I can't confirm that, so I lean toward no defect.

{"defective": false, "confidence": 0.55, "reason": "The trace edges are smooth with no visible open, short, mousebite or spur; the two small dark dots at the bottom edge are ambiguous and could be normal holes."}

The image is a binary rendering of a PCB with black traces on a white background. It shows two pads with traces, a circular arc at the top right, and a small round dot at the bottom left. The dot is probably a drilled hole or via. The traces look continuous, with no visible shorts, opens, spurs, or mousebites. The pad edges are smooth.

The isolated dot at the bottom left could be spurious copper, but its clean circular shape is more consistent with a hole or fiducial.

{"defective": false, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "Traces and pads are continuous with smooth edges and no visible open, short, mousebite or spur; the small round dot looks like an intentional hole."}

This binary image shows large black shapes on a white background, so the black areas are probably the copper or the pads. The small round hole at (350,133) is a circular feature, probably a via or a drilled hole, and it looks intentional. The shapes are clean, with no visible mousebites, spurs, or shorts. The top-left trace looks like a thin white line running around a black area, which seems normal.

I see no clear defect.

{"defective": false, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "Shapes have clean edges and the round hole looks like an intentional via; no visible open, short, mousebite, spur or spurious copper."}

This binary image shows a copper pad at the top with a small round hole. The hole is a round pin hole inside the copper pad, which could be a pin hole defect, though it may be a via or drill hole. Its position near the pad's top and its small size suggest it could be a pin hole defect. The bottom pad has a notch at the top, which is possibly a mousebite or a design feature.

The top hole is a clean circle inside the copper, so it is likely a pin hole defect. The notch at the bottom pad is rectangular, which is odd for a pad.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "A small circular void inside the top copper pad looks like a pin hole, and the rectangular notch in the bottom pad is also suspicious."}

I read the image as inverted: black is copper and white is substrate. Between the two left pads at about (185-235, 390), a thin black line connects the pad on the left to the pad on the right. That is a copper bridge, which is a short between two separate pads.

The image also shows a small black spur near (190, 138) and some thin white scratches, but the bridge is enough to call the board defective.

{"defective": true, "confidence": 0.88, "reason": "A thin copper bridge connects two adjacent pads at around (185-235, 392), forming a short; there is also a small spur near (190,138)."}
